            <ArticleTitle>Race-specific relationships between coronary and carotid artery calcification and carotid intimal medial thickness.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e97-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND AND PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Calcified arterial plaque has been proposed as a subclinical marker of atherosclerosis. We compared it to a well-validated surrogate--carotid intimal medial thickness (IMT).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Calcified arterial plaque was measured in 2 vascular beds (coronary and carotid) by computed tomography, and common carotid artery IMT was measured by B-mode ultrasonography, in 438 participants.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Calcium was positively associated with IMT (r=0.36 for coronary and r=0.45 for carotid, both P&lt;0.0001). Correlations were attenuated with adjustment for age, sex, and diabetes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Calcified plaque in the coronary and carotid arteries is moderately associated with subclinical atherosclerosis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
